  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nThis weekend... A low over far southern Saskatchewan near the \ninternational border will slowly track east towards Ontario \nthis weekend. This has us in the warm sector of this low and\ncontinued WAA aloft will help lift us into the 80s and maybe\neven some lower 90s for highs today. This will continue into\nSunday with more WAA until the cold front associated with this\nlow passes through late Sunday into early Monday. Areas that\nreach the diurnal maximum temperature before frontal passage on\nSunday (generally all but parts of western Minnesota) could see\nhighs near or above the current records. Our current forecast \nhas today falling short of record highs, but tomorrow breaking \nrecord highs. Today is forecast to be hotter, but tomorrow has \nlower records that are easier to break. For the 10th, the \nrecords are: MSP (90 in 1928), STC (88 in 1928), and EAU (88 in\n1928). For the 11th, the records are: MSP (85 in 2015), STC (83\nin 2015), and EAU (85 in 1930).\n\nAll this warmth will also promote ample mixing today which will\nprovide for a gusty late morning into early evening. The gusts \nwill be highest along the Buffalo Ridge in southwest Minnesota. \nIn addition we will warm enough today for RH values to fall into\nthe 30s with some upper 20s possible in western Minnesota. This\nwill provide for elevated fire weather concerns in western \nMinnesota. Thankfully we are still early enough in the fall that\nvegetation has not dried out and fuels are not as available as \nthey will be later in the season. So the biggest fire risk will \nbe agriculture related where crops have been dried out for \nharvesting. Due to this we are not expecting to issue a Red \nFlag Warning unless RH values come in much lower than expected.\n\nNext week... Monday will see temperatures come down from the\nweekend's near record highs as we move into the post-frontal air\nmass. There could also be some showers Monday afternoon as a\nvery subtle shortwave feature should move across North Dakota \ninto northern Minnesota. What rain chances occur with this are \nbest to our north, but parts of central Minnesota could also see\nsome light rain with this. Overall temperatures look to stay \nnear normal over the week ahead of the next system passing \nthrough on Thursday. Behind this system temperature look to be \nbelow normal with highs only in the mid 50s. How good of a \nchance for rain we have though varies between the global \ndeterministic models. The evolution of the upper air pattern has\ntwo paths with one ejecting the trough over the Upper Midwest \n(this gives us a good chance for a soaking rain) and the other \nkeeps more of a cut off low over the western CONUS (this would \nkeep us on the dry side). There is also a greater spread the the\nensemble solutions than we saw 24 hours ago. So continued \nconfidence in colder more fall like air, but lower confidence in\nThursday's rain chance.",
